    "summary": "Lively early‑'90 set with fiery Little Red Rooster and strong Wharf Rat",
    "blurb": "Brent’s scorching Little Red Rooster solo and a solid Estimated Prophet lead the first half, while the second set gains steam after the Drums with The Wheel, Gimme Some Lovin' and an epic Wharf Rat. Jerry delivers crisp leads throughout, and Bob adds vocal punch on Estimated Prophet. Overall the performance is energetic and consistently engaging.",
    "review": "Feb 25‑1990 at the Oakland‑Alameda County Coliseum captures a springtime Dead that’s both tight and adventurous. The first set opens with the crowd‑pleasing “Touch of Grey” before diving into bluesy territory on “Little Red Rooster,” where Brent Mydland erupts in a blistering solo that many reviewers liken to Willie Dixon‑style slide work. Though a few songs feel a touch rushed, Jerry Garcia stays \"on top of his game,\" especially during the soaring lead on “Eyes Of The World.”\n\nThe second set truly lifts off after the drum duet: “The Wheel” rolls into a soulful “Gimme Some Lovin',” which segues seamlessly into an expansive “Wharf Rat” that serves as the night’s emotional high point. Highlights also include a tight “Estimated Prophet,” a joyous “Just A Little Light,” and a jubilant encore with “Knockin’ On Heaven’s Door.” Across multiple sources the show is praised for its lively energy, strong keyboard work, and overall solid band cohesion, making this performance a standout entry from the early ’90s era.\n\nFor listeners seeking the clearest picture of the night, Charlie Miller’s SBD remix (gd1990-02-25.sbd.walker‑scotton.miller.87786.sbeok.flac16) is the recommended source – it balances crisp instrument separation with just enough crowd ambience to feel alive.",
